who benefits from consumer relief  payouts Citigroup has agreed to pay  seven billion dollars to settle  government claims over fraudulent  activity in the mortgage security market  in 2006 and 2007 conduct that Attorney  General Eric Holder describes as  egregious late last year JPMorgan  settled similar allegations for 13  billion dollars with four billion  earmarked for consumer relief two and a  half billion of this latest Citigroup  fine will fund similar efforts the  beneficiaries will include underwater  homeowners will be able to modify their  existing loans distressed borrowers will  get help with refinancing home buyers  will be eligible for assistance with  down payments and closing costs and  community focused organizations will  receive donations to help with  redevelopment and affordable housing  programs the main aim of course is to  keep citigroup borrowers in their homes  the precedent for both these consumer  relief programs is a 25 billion dollar  mortgage-related settlement between the  federal government 49 states and five  major banks including JPMorgan and  Citigroup just a year after that deal  was struck in 2012 the five banks  reported to government monitors that  more than 640,000 borrowers at each  enjoyed benefits totaling around $80,000  the Department of Justice says these  fines an attempt to hold institutions  accountable but they should also offer  some relief to those whose lives have  been negatively affected by Wall Street
 

